@meta
id: 28-05
title: Search that guesses the case right
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 98
minutes: 6
vscode: the Aa toggle in the find widget, clicked on and off all day
open: .vimrc payment.service.ts
vimrc: set ignorecase | set smartcase

@brief
Two settings, and they only make sense together.

    set ignorecase            " /refund finds Refund
    set smartcase             " ...but /Refund finds only Refund

On its own `ignorecase` is annoying: you can no longer search for an
exact capitalised name. `smartcase` fixes that by reading your pattern —
all lowercase means you did not care about case, a single capital means
you did. The result is that you stop thinking about case at all, which is
the point of a good default.

Add both to the Searching section of the vimrc. Then use them: the second
buffer has a method called `legacyRefund` that finance now call the
reversal. Rename that one method to `reverseCharge`, both where it is
declared and where it is called, and find it by typing the name in
lowercase. Nothing else in either file changes.

Both settings are already live in this session, so the search works
before you save anything. That is deliberate — you are writing down a
behaviour you have just felt.

@keys
| `/pattern` `Enter` | search forward |
| `n` | next match |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Write the config first, `:w`, then `:bn` to the TypeScript file.
--
`/legacyrefund` finds `legacyRefund` because of `ignorecase`. `ciw`
replaces the word under the cursor; `n` gets you to the second one.
--
Two occurrences: the declaration and the call inside `reverse`. `ciw`
the first, `n` to the next, then `.` repeats the change.

@solution
    /hlsearch<Enter> o set ignorecase<Enter> set smartcase <Esc> :w<Enter>
    :bn<Enter> /legacyrefund<Enter> ciw reverseCharge <Esc> n . :wqa<Enter>

`.` repeats the whole change, which is why the second rename costs one
keystroke. That combination — search, change, `n`, `.` — is the reason
`ignorecase` earns its place. You type the name the way it sounds in your
head rather than the way the codebase capitalised it.

`smartcase` has one edge that surprises people: it applies to `/` and `?`,
but not to `:substitute`. `:%s/refund/x/g` still ignores case if
`ignorecase` is on, regardless of the capitals in your pattern. When a
substitution must be exact, put `\C` in the pattern. Chapter 13 made you
learn `\C` for exactly this moment.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Searching ---
set incsearch
set hlsearch

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Searching ---
set incsearch
set hlsearch
set ignorecase
set smartcase

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    return this.gateway.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });
  }

  public async legacyRefund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.gateway.post(`/v1/reversals`, { orderId, amount });
  }

  public async reverse(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.legacyRefund(orderId, amount);
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    return this.gateway.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });
  }

  public async reverseCharge(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.gateway.post(`/v1/reversals`, { orderId, amount });
  }

  public async reverse(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.reverseCharge(orderId, amount);
  }
}
